    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_claude`. Both tests pass: the existing status test and a new test for the Claude client.\n\n**One thing you should know:** the installed `anthropic==1.8.0` runs on `httpx2`, not `httpx`, and it rejects an `httpx.Client` with a TypeError. So I couldn't pass it the client from `build_http_client()` directly. Instead:\n\n- **`app/http.py`**: I added `build_httpx2_client(transport=None)` next to `build_http_client`. It builds an `httpx2.Client` with the same settings, reusing `EGRESS_TAG`, `CONNECT_TIMEOUT`, `READ_TIMEOUT` and the same `log_egress` hook. The one thing it doesn't reuse is the `TIMEOUT` object, because that's an `httpx.Timeout`. It builds an `httpx2.Timeout` from the same two constants instead. `build_http_client` is unchanged, so `fetch_status` works as before.\n- **`app/claude.py`**: `build_claude` creates that client, passing `transport` through, and hands it to `Anthropic(api_key=..., max_retries=2, timeout=..., http_client=...)`. I pass the timeout explicitly so the SDK can't fall back to its default of 600 seconds. I checked the SDK source: each attempt, retries included, goes through the client's `send`, so every request gets the header and one log line.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_claude.py`. It uses an `httpx2.MockTransport` that returns 529 twice and then 200. It confirms that all 3 requests carry `X-Egress-Tag`, that there are exactly 3 `app.egress` log lines, and that the timeouts are 5 s connect and 60 s read. Your own tests will need to pass an `httpx2` transport, such as `httpx2.MockTransport`. An `httpx` transport will be rejected.\n\n`httpx2` is already installed because `anthropic` depends on it, so nothing new was installed. But `app/http.py` now imports it directly, and it isn't listed in `pyproject.toml`. I left `pyproject.toml` alone as instructed. If you want direct imports declared, `httpx2` would need to be added there."
  },
